    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change fits the existing structure cleanly:\n\n- **Right unit.** The controller already owns a family of pre-persist guards (`validateRequiredFields`, `rejectDailyLimitReached`, `rejectCityAtCapacity`, `rejectDuplicateIdentity`). A date-validity guard belongs alongside them, not pushed into the mapper or service.\n- **Naming matches convention.** `rejectFutureRegistrationDate` follows the established `reject\u2026` guard-method naming already used in this class.\n- **No duplication / no catch-all.** It reuses the existing `InvalidOwnerFieldsException` with the field-name-list contract exactly as `validateRequiredFields` does, so the 400 mapping and error shape stay consistent. Nothing is reinvented.\n- **Sensible placement and ordering.** The method sits next to the other private helpers, and the call in `addOwner` is correctly positioned to validate the raw supplied value before `toBusinessDay` adjusts it \u2014 which the Javadoc also documents, matching the surrounding methods' documentation style.\n\nA maintainer of this codebase would find this natural.",
